From fa9e4bf4591b61d0565fc5826235216598e82ea7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:56:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] Make the video cover the user's choice
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`thumbnail_path` was written once by the renderer — one frame grabbed at
1.0s — and never again. It is the image the library grid, the share
preview and every platform that embeds the video shows before anyone
presses play, and the user had no say in it.

Three routes, converging on PATCH .../cover as the only place the column
is writable from outside the renderer:

  POST .../cover-candidates           an even spread of frames, free
  POST .../cover-candidates/generate  purpose-made covers, on request
  PATCH .../cover                     set the chosen one

Frames and generated covers fail in opposite directions, so both exist.
An extracted frame is free and always available, but motion-blurred,
compression-damaged, and locked to the video's aspect — cropping a 9:16
frame to 16:9 leaves 1080x608, which either beheads the subject or leaves
them unreadable at thumbnail size. A generated cover is composed for the
shape it will be shown in, and can carry the exaggerated expression a
cover wants and a talking-head video must not have. It costs image
generations, so nothing generates unless the user asks.

Generated covers are anchored to the same outfit reference the video's
scenes were, with requireReferenceInput, so a cover is provably the same
person in the same clothes as the video it fronts. The setting comes from
the first scene's visual rather than being invented.

Uploading your own cover needs no new endpoint: the presigned upload flow
already exists, and PATCH .../cover accepts any image path this tenant
owns. That path is validated with the same canonical check the guided
visuals use, and the body is checked against an explicit one-key
allowlist BEFORE the generated parse, because the generated parser strips
unknown keys and returns success — a boundary taking a storage path has
to prove the rejection rather than assume it.

The chosen cover propagates to the library copy when the video has been
saved, since that is the row the grid actually reads.

VideoJob gains `coverGeneratable`, so the rule for whether covers CAN be
generated lives on the server next to the route that enforces it, and a
client can never offer a button the route would refuse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
